.TH LIFELINE 8 "2026-11-20" "lifeline 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
lifeline \- Wi-Fi-aware Ethernet DHCP router and failover service
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lifeline
manages the Ethernet interface on a laptop with Wi-Fi and Ethernet so it acts
either as a local DHCP/NAT gateway or as a failover DHCP client:
.TP
.B PRIMARY
Wi-Fi is healthy and no other DHCP server is active on Ethernet. Ethernet uses
its static address from config, serves DHCPv4/SLAAC/DHCPv6/DNS from
.I /etc/lifeline/dhcp_hosts.csv
(plus an optional fallback
.I dynamic_pool
if configured), and forwards IPv4/IPv6 traffic over Wi-Fi and active VPN/tunnel
interfaces (such as Tailscale or VMware tunnels) for active DHCP clients only.
.TP
.B SECONDARY
Wi-Fi is healthy, but another DHCP server (router or another lifeline laptop)
is already active on Ethernet. This laptop remains on standby using Wi-Fi and
promotes only if the primary server disappears.
.TP
.B FAILOVER
Wi-Fi connectivity failed. DHCP serving and Ethernet forwarding stop, and
Ethernet requests an IP, gateway, and DNS via DHCP.
.TP
.B ELECTION
Wi-Fi is healthy and no primary DHCP server was found; waiting a brief
priority, Wi-Fi probe latency, and MAC-ordered delay before promoting to
.BR PRIMARY .
.TP
.B IDLE
Ethernet link carrier is down (cable unplugged).
.SH SERVICE CONTROL
.TP
.B sudo systemctl start lifeline
Start the service.
.TP
.B sudo systemctl stop lifeline
Stop the service, halt DHCP serving, and restore Ethernet/firewall state.
.TP
.B sudo systemctl restart lifeline
Restart the daemon (required after changing interfaces or daemon settings).
.TP
.B sudo systemctl reload lifeline
Hot-reload
.I config.toml
and
.I dhcp_hosts.csv
without restarting the service.
.TP
.B sudo systemctl enable \-\-now lifeline
Enable at boot and start immediately.
.SH CHECKING STATUS AND SERVED CLIENTS
.TP
.B sudo lifelinectl status
Shows the current mode, reason, detected interfaces, active uplinks (Wi-Fi/VPN),
Wi-Fi probe RTT, other lifeline peers on the switch, and all active DHCP
client leases.
.TP
.B lifelinectl prompt [\-\-bash|\-\-zsh] [\-\-color]
Prints a compact badge (for example
.BR [LL:PRI(2)] )
for shell
.B PS1
prompts without requiring root. On desktop sessions,
.B lifeline-tray
also provides a menu-bar indicator and mode-change notifications.
.TP
.B sudo nft list table inet lifeline
Shows which client IP/MAC pairs are currently allowed to forward traffic.
.TP
.B cat /run/lifeline/dnsmasq.leases
Lists current DHCP leases issued when in
.B PRIMARY
mode.
.SH LOGS AND DEBUGGING
Lifeline logs to
.BR systemd-journald
(and
.I /var/log/syslog
if
.B rsyslog
is installed).
.TP
.B journalctl -u lifeline
Show full daemon log history.
.TP
.B journalctl -u lifeline | grep Transitioning
List every mode change and why it happened.
.TP
.B journalctl -u lifeline -f
Follow mode changes and probe events in real time.
.TP
.B journalctl -t dnsmasq
View DHCP server requests, offers, and lease assignments.
.PP
To enable verbose debug logging temporarily:
.RS
.EX
sudo systemctl edit lifeline
# Add under [Service]:
#   Environment=LIFELINE_LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG
sudo systemctl restart lifeline
.EE
.RE
.PP
Common checks when DHCP is not being served:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Mode is SECONDARY:
Check
.I ext_dhcp
and
.I peers
in
.B lifelinectl status
to see which router or peer laptop owns the segment.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Mode is FAILOVER:
Check
.I Wi-Fi Health
in
.B lifelinectl status
and
.BR journalctl\ -u\ lifeline .
.IP \(bu 2
.B Client gets no IP in PRIMARY:
Verify the client MAC is listed in
.I /etc/lifeline/dhcp_hosts.csv
and run
.BR sudo\ lifelinectl\ validate .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/lifeline/config.toml
Main configuration file.
.TP
.I /etc/lifeline/config.env
Optional environment overrides (for example
.BR ETH_IP ).
.TP
.I /etc/lifeline/dhcp_hosts.csv
Static hardware-MAC-to-IP table.
.TP
.I /etc/lifeline/hosts.d/*.csv
Optional drop-in MAC-to-IP tables.
.TP
.I /run/lifeline/status.json
Live status snapshot updated every second.
.SH AUTHOR
Vikram Aggarwal
.SH REPORTING BUGS AND CONTRIBUTING
Bug fixes, hardware-compatibility notes, and feature improvements are warmly
encouraged. When reporting an issue, include output from
.B sudo lifelinectl status \-\-json
and
.BR journalctl\ -u\ lifeline\ -b .
